# Architecture Guard

Apply this rule silently to every generated or modified code path.

## Memory and Resource Safety

- Identify the owner and lifetime of each allocation, pointer, handle, file
  descriptor, socket, lock, thread, subprocess, mapping, and temporary file.
- Check allocation and system-call results before use.
- Prove pointer arithmetic and indexing stay inside the originating object.
- Use overflow-safe size calculations before allocation, copying, or offset
  movement.
- Pair every acquisition with explicit cleanup on success, failure, timeout,
  cancellation, and partial initialization.
- Prefer scoped ownership, RAII, context managers, and one auditable cleanup
  path. Reject double release, use-after-free, leaks, dangling references, and
  hidden ownership transfer.

## Complexity

- Determine time and auxiliary-space complexity for every nontrivial path.
- Flag data-dependent nested traversal as a potential quadratic defect.
- Replace unexpected `O(n^2)` work with indexing, hashing, sorting plus linear
  scan, heaps, graph algorithms, dynamic programming, batching, or streaming as
  appropriate.
- Permit quadratic work only when required by the problem or bounded by a small
  documented invariant.
- Reject repeated parsing, copying, allocation, I/O, queries, and full scans
  inside hot loops.

## Architecture

- Keep parsing, domain logic, persistence, transport, orchestration, and
  presentation behind cohesive interfaces.
- Enforce dependency direction; core logic must not depend on delivery details.
- Prefer small pure functions and explicit state over global mutable state.
- Validate inputs and propagate contextual errors without swallowing failures.
- Preserve public contracts and compatibility unless the request changes them.
- Make dependencies injectable or otherwise controllable in tests.

## Verification

- Cover normal, empty, boundary, malformed, dependency-failure, cleanup, and
  regression paths.
- Run the cheapest relevant static check before the authoritative test suite.
- Never claim safety, complexity, coverage, or correctness without evidence.
- Surface only actionable violations with `path:line`, consequence, and repair.
